The C program is small — read one int with [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m, then print [38;5;10mi j[0m pairs for [38;5;10mi[0m in [38;5;10m0..x[0m with [38;5;10mj = 2i[0m. The translation work was almost entirely in faithfully reproducing [38;5;10mscanf[0m and integer edge cases:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mscanf[0m failure leaves [38;5;10mx[0m unchanged.** On empty input, [38;5;10mabc[0m, a bare [38;5;10m-[0m, etc., [38;5;10mx[0m stays [38;5;10m0[0m and nothing prints. I return a bool from [38;5;10mscan_int[0m and simply ignore it, mirroring the C which ignores [38;5;10mscanf[0m's return value.[0m[0m
- **Whitespace skipping crosses newlines,** and the first non-matching byte is pushed back rather than consumed — hence the one-byte-pushback reader instead of line-based input.[0m[0m
- **Overflow clamping.** glibc routes [38;5;10m%d[0m through [38;5;10mstrtol[0m, clamping to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m and [3mthen[23m narrowing to [38;5;10mint[0m. My first attempt saturated the magnitude and negated afterwards, which diverged on [38;5;10m-99999999999999999999[0m: that path yields [38;5;10mx == 1[0m (prints one line) where the C prints nothing. Accumulating the magnitude and applying the clamp after the sign fixes it.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mj += 2[0m uses [38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m** so a huge [38;5;10mx[0m wraps like the C rather than panicking in debug.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Verification: differential test against [38;5;10mgcc -O2[0m on the original, covering signs, garbage, trailing garbage, leading whitespace and newlines, [38;5;10mi64[0m/[38;5;10mu64[0m/[38;5;10mi32[0m boundary values, plus 500 random inputs from a digit/sign/whitespace alphabet and full-output diffs up to [38;5;10mx = 999999[0m. All matched. The temporary C binary was removed; [38;5;10mc_src/[0m is untouched.[0m[0m
